1. Ke3 {The king, at his third square.} Ke5 {The king, at his fourth square.} 2. Kf3 {The king,
at his bishop's third square.} Kf5 {The king, at his bishop's fourth square.} 3. Kg3 {The king,
at his knight's third square.} Ke5 {The king, at his fourth square. (a) NOTE: In this position,
if the black were to play first, the white would win the game.} 4. Kg4 {The king, at his
knight's fourth square.} Kf6 {The king, at his bishop's third square.} 5. Kg3 {The king, at his
knight's third square.} Ke5 {The king, at his fourth square. (c) NOTE: Had he played his king to
his bishop's fourth square, you would have got again the the move over him; which would have
given you the game.} 6. Kf3 {The king, at his bishop's third square.} Kf5 {The king, at his
bishop's fourth square. (d) NOTE: As he don't push his pawn, nor give you leave to take the
place opposite to his king, it must be a drawn-game.} *
